  from jsonb_array_elements('[{"n":35,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A seed packet claims that 85% of its seeds germinate. A gardener plants 20 seeds and 14 germinate.\n\n(a) Calculate the relative frequency of germination in the gardener''s trial.\n(b) The gardener concludes the packet''s claim is false. Explain why this conclusion is unsafe for a sample of 20.\n(c) Calculate how many of the 20 seeds you would expect to germinate if the claim were true, and use it to judge the gardener''s result.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A seed packet claims that 85% of its seeds germinate. A gardener plants 20 seeds and 14 germinate."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the relative frequency of germination in the gardener''s trial.\n(b) The gardener concludes the packet''s claim is false. Explain why this conclusion is unsafe for a sample of 20.\n(c) Calculate how many of the 20 seeds you would expect to germinate if the claim were true, and use it to judge the gardener''s result."}]}]},"e":"The observed relative frequency is 0.70 against a claim of 0.85, an expected 17 of 20. A shortfall of 3 is within ordinary sampling variation for such a small sample, so the claim is not disproved; a trial of several hundred seeds would be needed.","m":5,"df":"hard","tp":"probability","st":"probability","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":35,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"Judge a probability claim against the expected count AND the size of the sample; small samples vary widely.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate the relative frequency of germination, as a decimal.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Divide the germinated seeds by the number planted.","14 ÷ 20."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":0.7,"tolerance":0.001},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the number of seeds expected to germinate if the claim is true.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Multiply the claimed probability by the 20 seeds.","0.85 × 20."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":17,"tolerance":0},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Explain why a result of 14 does not disprove the claim.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["Compare the observed 14 with the expected 17.","Would a different handful of 20 seeds give a different count?"],"strand":"D(iii)","accepted":["14 is only 3 below the expected 17, and with a sample of only 20 that shortfall is ordinary random variation, so a larger trial is needed before rejecting the claim","the sample is too small: random variation in 20 seeds easily produces a difference of 3"],"keywords":[[["standard deviation","spread","variation","variability","consisten"],["smaller","larger","closer","more","less","compare","mean"]],[["sample","small","few","20 spins","20 seeds","200 spins","variation","chance"],["too small","random","not enough","larger","more trials","expected","difference"]]],"minKeywordGroups":1}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark for 17. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the comparison, 1 for sampling variation, 1 for the recommendation of a larger sample.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark for 17. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the comparison, 1 for sampling variation, 1 for the recommendation of a larger sample.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Quote the expected count in (c) — the judgement is a comparison with it, not an opinion."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark for 17. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the comparison, 1 for sampling variation, 1 for the recommendation of a larger sample."},{"n":36,"t":"assess_parts","p":"In a survey of 50 students about two sports, 24 play football (F), 20 play hockey (H) and 9 play both.\n\n(a) Calculate how many play football only.\n(b) Calculate how many play exactly one of the two sports.\n(c) State how many play neither, and describe the region (F ∪ H)′ in words.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"In a survey of 50 students about two sports, 24 play football (F), 20 play hockey (H) and 9 play both."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate how many play football only.\n(b) Calculate how many play exactly one of the two sports.\n(c) State how many play neither, and describe the region (F ∪ H)′ in words."}]}]},"e":"Overlap 9, football only 15, hockey only 11, so exactly one sport = 26 and the union is 35. Neither = 50 − 35 = 15. The complement of the union is exactly that ''neither'' region.","m":7,"df":"hard","tp":"sets and Venn diagrams","st":"sets and Venn diagrams","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":36,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"Fill the overlap first; ''exactly one'' means the two ''only'' regions and excludes the overlap.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate how many students play football only.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["The 24 footballers include everyone who plays both sports.","Subtract the 9 who play both from the 24."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":15,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate how many students play exactly one of the two sports.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Add the football-only and hockey-only regions.","(24 − 9) + (20 − 9)."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":26,"tolerance":0},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate how many students play neither sport.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["First add all three regions inside the circles.","Subtract that union from 50."],"strand":"D(iii)","answer":15,"tolerance":0},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Describe (F ∪ H)′ in words.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["The dash means ''the complement'': everything outside.","Outside both circles means neither sport."],"strand":"D(iv)","accepted":["students who play neither football nor hockey"],"keywords":[["students"],["play"],["neither"],["football"],["hockey"]],"minKeywordGroups":3}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for both only-regions, 1 for 26. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the union 35, 1 for 15. (d) 2 marks: 1 for ''neither'', 1 for describing it as outside both sets.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for both only-regions, 1 for 26. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the union 35, 1 for 15. (d) 2 marks: 1 for ''neither'', 1 for describing it as outside both sets.","response_tools":["venn","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"venn","response_guidance":"Place the 9 in the overlap first, then the two ''only'' regions, then the 15 outside the circles.","response_venn":{"title":"Football and hockey","instructions":"Construct the two-set diagram. Put the overlap in first, then the only-regions and the students outside both circles.","allow_set_change":false,"starter":{"sets":2,"labels":["Football","Hockey"],"universalLabel":"Students"}},"response_default_tool":"venn"},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for both only-regions, 1 for 26. (c) 2 marks: 1 for the union 35, 1 for 15. (d) 2 marks: 1 for ''neither'', 1 for describing it as outside both sets."},{"n":37,"t":"assess_parts","p":"Six identical machines take 10 days to complete an order.\n\n(a) Calculate the total number of machine-days needed for the order.\n(b) Calculate how many days four machines would take at the same rate.\n(c) After 4 days, two more machines are added to the original six. Calculate how many further days are needed.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Six identical machines take 10 days to complete an order."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the total number of machine-days needed for the order.\n(b) Calculate how many days four machines would take at the same rate.\n(c) After 4 days, two more machines are added to the original six. Calculate how many further days are needed."}]}]},"e":"The order is 60 machine-days. Four machines take 15 days. In 4 days the six machines finish 24 machine-days, leaving 36; eight machines then need 36 ÷ 8 = 4.5 further days.","m":8,"df":"hard","tp":"proportion","st":"proportion","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":37,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"In inverse-proportion work problems, convert to machine-days (or person-days) first, then divide by the new rate.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate the total number of machine-days needed.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["One machine working for one day completes one machine-day of work.","Six machines working for ten days: multiply the two numbers."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":60,"tolerance":0,"unit":"machine-days"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate how many days four machines would take.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["The order still needs the same total machine-days of work.","With four machines, divide the machine-days by four."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":15,"tolerance":0,"unit":"days"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the work completed in the first 4 days, in machine-days.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Count the machine-days completed before the extra machines arrive.","Six machines each work for four days."],"strand":"D(iii)","answer":24,"tolerance":0,"unit":"machine-days"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Calculate how many further days the eight machines need.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["Subtract 24 from 60 to find the work left.","With two extra machines there are 8 working at once: divide by 8."],"strand":"D(iv)","answer":4.5,"tolerance":0.05,"unit":"days"}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the division, 1 for 15. (c) 2 marks: 1 for 6 × 4, 1 for 24. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the remaining 36, 1 for using 8 machines, 1 for 4.5.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the division, 1 for 15. (c) 2 marks: 1 for 6 × 4, 1 for 24. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the remaining 36, 1 for using 8 machines, 1 for 4.5.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Work in machine-days throughout; never add the days of different machines as if they were separate."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the division, 1 for 15. (c) 2 marks: 1 for 6 × 4, 1 for 24. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the remaining 36, 1 for using 8 machines, 1 for 4.5."},{"n":38,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A charity lottery sells 800 tickets. One ticket wins $200 and five tickets win $20 each. A ticket costs $2.\n\n(a) Write the probability of winning the $200 prize as a decimal.\n(b) Calculate the expected winnings from one ticket.\n(c) A supporter argues that buying 10 tickets makes the game fair. Evaluate this argument using expected values.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A charity lottery sells 800 tickets. One ticket wins "},{"type":"text","text":"$200 and five tickets win $"},{"type":"text","text":"20 each. A ticket costs "},{"type":"text","text":"$2."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Write the probability of winning the "},{"type":"text","text":"$200 prize as a decimal.\n(b) Calculate the expected winnings from one ticket.\n(c) A supporter argues that buying 10 tickets makes the game fair. Evaluate this argument using expected values."}]}]},"e":"Expected winnings per ticket = 0.00125(200) + 0.00625(20) = $0.375 against a $2 cost, an expected loss of $1.625 per ticket. Ten tickets cost $20 and return $3.75 in expectation, so the game remains firmly unfair.","m":6,"df":"hard","tp":"probability","st":"probability","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":38,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"Expected value is linear: multiplying the number of tickets multiplies both the cost and the expectation, leaving the expected loss per ticket unchanged.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Write the probability of winning the $200 prize as a decimal.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["One ticket out of 800.","1 ÷ 800."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":0.00125,"tolerance":1e-05},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the expected winnings from one ticket, in dollars.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Multiply each prize by its probability and add.","0.00125 × 200 + 0.00625 × 20."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":0.375,"tolerance":0.005,"unit":"$"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Evaluate the argument that 10 tickets make the game fair.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["What do 10 tickets cost?","How does multiplying the tickets affect the expected winnings?"],"strand":"D(iii)","accepted":["the argument fails: 10 tickets cost $20 and return only $3.75 in expectation, a loss of $16.25, and buying more tickets scales the cost and the expected winnings equally so the game stays unfair"],"keywords":[["argument"],["fails"],["tickets"],["cost"],["return"],["expectation"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the products, 1 for $0.375. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the cost of 10 tickets, 1 for the expected return, 1 for the conclusion that scaling does not make it fair.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the products, 1 for $0.375. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the cost of 10 tickets, 1 for the expected return, 1 for the conclusion that scaling does not make it fair.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"In (c) compute the cost AND the expected return for 10 tickets; the comparison is the argument."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the products, 1 for $0.375. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the cost of 10 tickets, 1 for the expected return, 1 for the conclusion that scaling does not make it fair."},{"n":39,"t":"assess_parts","p":"Two students simplify the ratio 45 : 60 : 75.\n\nAisha writes 3 : 4 : 5. Ben writes 9 : 12 : 15.\n\n(a) State the highest common factor of 45, 60 and 75.\n(b) Explain which of the two answers is fully simplified and why the other is not wrong but incomplete.\n(c) The ratio represents the prize money of three winners sharing $540. Calculate each winner''s share using the simplified ratio.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Two students simplify the ratio 45 : 60 : 75."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Aisha writes 3 : 4 : 5. Ben writes 9 : 12 : 15."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the highest common factor of 45, 60 and 75.\n(b) Explain which of the two answers is fully simplified and why the other is not wrong but incomplete.\n(c) The ratio represents the prize money of three winners sharing "},{"type":"text","text":"$540. Calculate each winner''s share using the simplified ratio."}]}]},"e":"Dividing 45 : 60 : 75 by 15 gives 3 : 4 : 5, so Aisha is fully simplified while Ben stopped at 9 : 12 : 15. The parts total 12, one part is $45, so the shares are $135, $180 and $225.","m":6,"df":"hard","tp":"ratio","st":"ratio","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp2-mock","question_number":39,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"Divide by the HCF, not just any common factor, and check that no common factor remains.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp2-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the highest common factor of 45, 60 and 75.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["List the factors of 45 and look for the largest one shared by all three.","15 divides all three; is any larger number a factor of all three?"],"strand":"C(i)","answer":15,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Explain which answer is fully simplified, and why the other is not wrong.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Which ratio cannot be divided by a common factor any more?","Equivalent ratios can be correct without being simplest form."],"strand":"C(ii)","accepted":["aisha''s 3 : 4 : 5 is fully simplified; ben''s 9 : 12 : 15 is an equivalent ratio but still divisible by 3, so it is not in simplest form"],"keywords":[["aisha''s"],["fully"],["simplified"],["ben''s"],["equivalent"],["ratio"]],"minKeywordGroups":4},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the value of one part, in dollars.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["The simplified parts total 3 + 4 + 5 = 12.","Divide $540 by 12."],"strand":"C(iii)","answer":45,"tolerance":0,"unit":"$"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"State the largest share, in dollars.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["The largest share is 5 parts.","Multiply your part value by 5."],"strand":"C(iii)","answer":225,"tolerance":0,"unit":"$"}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for naming Aisha''s ratio, 1 for explaining equivalence. (c) 1 mark for $45. (d) 2 marks: 1 for 5 parts, 1 for $225.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for naming Aisha''s ratio, 1 for explaining equivalence. (c) 1 mark for $45. (d) 2 marks: 1 for 5 parts, 1 for $225.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Show the part value in (c); both shares follow from it, and it earns its own mark."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for naming Aisha''s ratio, 1 for explaining equivalence. (c) 1 mark for $45. (d) 2 marks: 1 for 5 parts, 1 for $225."}]'::jsonb) as x
